posts=[]
def post(slug,title,category,description,sections):
    posts.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,category=category,description=description,date='2026-09-09',read_time=f'{max(3,round(sum(len(t.split()) for h,t in sections)/180))} min read',sections=[{'heading':h,'body':t} for h,t in sections],published=True))
post('custom-crm-development-cost','How much does it cost to build a custom CRM?','CRM','Understand the decisions that shape a custom CRM estimate, from workflows and integrations to migration and maintenance.',[
('Start with the workflow, not a price list','A useful CRM estimate begins with how your team finds, qualifies and serves customers. A system for a small team tracking leads and follow-ups is a different project from one connecting sales, support, invoicing and several external services. List the people who will use it, the decisions they make and the records they need. That gives a developer a concrete basis for scope.'),
('What changes the development effort','Custom pipeline stages, granular permissions, recurring tasks, reporting and approval rules all affect effort. Integrations add another dimension: an API may be straightforward, but handling failures, duplicate events and incomplete data still takes work. Data migration needs its own assessment, especially when existing spreadsheets contain inconsistent names or duplicate customers.'),
('Define a useful first release','Separate essential workflows from improvements that can follow. A focused first release might include lead capture, customer records, ownership, next actions and a simple pipeline. Advanced automation can follow once the team has used the core workflow. This approach makes scope easier to explain and reduces the risk of investing in features that do not fit everyday work.'),
('Ask for a transparent estimate','A project proposal should state the included workflows, assumptions, dependencies, acceptance criteria and how changes will be handled. Ask about deployment, migration, training and post-launch support as well as development. A responsible estimate is specific to the project; a universal fixed price would hide too many assumptions.'),
('Prepare for a project discussion','Bring sample spreadsheets with sensitive information removed, a description of your current sales process and the names of services the CRM must connect with. Explain what is most frustrating today and what a successful first release would change. These inputs are more useful than a long list of features without context.')])
post('custom-software-instead-of-excel','When your business outgrows spreadsheets','Business Software','Recognize when a shared spreadsheet should become a structured application, and how to approach the transition.',[
('Spreadsheets are a useful starting point','A spreadsheet is a flexible way to explore a process and organize small amounts of information. The question is whether the workflow still fits the tool. If people spend more time reconciling copies, repairing formulas or asking who changed a record than doing the underlying work, it may be time to review the system.'),
('Look for workflow problems','Repeated entry, unclear ownership, missing approvals and inconsistent access are signs of a process that needs more structure. A custom application can validate inputs, relate records and guide users through defined steps. Software will not resolve unclear business rules by itself, so document those rules before replacing the spreadsheet.'),
('Migrate one process at a time','Choose a workflow with a clear owner and measurable pain. Clean its source data, define the required fields and agree on the new source of truth. A staged migration lets the team verify the application against familiar records before more processes depend on it. Keep a controlled backup of the original data.'),
('Measure practical outcomes','Decide what you want to observe after launch: fewer duplicate records, faster retrieval, clearer approvals or less repeated entry. Establish a baseline before migration. This makes the business discussion about actual operational changes rather than a general promise that custom software will save time.')])
post('laravel-for-saas-applications','Choosing a foundation for your SaaS product','SaaS Development','A practical guide to evaluating Laravel for a SaaS application around your team, tenancy model and operating needs.',[
('Choose around the product','The right technical foundation depends on the product, the team maintaining it and the environment where it will run. For a business application with accounts, forms, permissions and background work, Laravel is one option to evaluate. Avoid choosing a framework only because it appears in a technology comparison.'),
('Make tenant boundaries explicit','A multi-tenant product needs a deliberate approach to organization identity and data access. Decide how requests identify a tenant, how records are scoped and what administrators can access. Tests should cover cross-tenant access attempts as well as normal workflows. A tenant identifier alone is not a complete isolation strategy.'),
('Design for operations early','Subscription events, email delivery, imports and external services create work beyond the request-response cycle. Plan retries, observability and handling for repeated events. Think about backups, deployment and support access before the first customer needs them. Operational clarity is part of product quality.'),
('Compare realistic alternatives','Evaluate alternatives against the same requirements: existing team knowledge, integration needs, hosting constraints and expected maintenance. Build a small representative workflow if a key requirement remains uncertain. This article is a planning guide, not a claim that one framework is faster or better for every SaaS product.')])
post('custom-hrms-employee-management','What a useful HRMS should do for your team','HRMS','Plan an HRMS around employee records, attendance exceptions, leave policies and reviewable payroll inputs.',[
('Connect the employee record','Employee information becomes more useful when attendance, leave and payroll reference the same person and employment context. Establish which records are authoritative and who can change them. Sensitive information requires careful access rules, and employees may need a different view from managers or payroll administrators.'),
('Write down policy exceptions','A policy that sounds simple can behave differently across shifts, holidays and partial-day leave. Gather examples from actual operations, including exceptions and corrections. Agree on effective dates for policy changes and how historical records should be treated. These decisions should guide development and acceptance checks.'),
('Make payroll inputs reviewable','Attendance events should not become unexplained salary numbers. The team needs to understand how attendance, leave and approved adjustments contribute to a payroll run. Provide a deliberate review step and a way to investigate discrepancies before finalization. The exact workflow should reflect the organization’s agreed policies.'),
('Roll out with the people who use it','Test representative records with HR and payroll staff before relying on the new system. Include overnight shifts, missing punches and changed leave decisions where relevant. A short, controlled parallel review can expose assumptions that ordinary happy-path examples miss.')])
post('warehouse-software-inventory-accuracy','Better inventory starts with traceable movements','Business Software','How warehouse workflows, barcodes and cycle counts support a more reliable view of stock.',[
('Record the movement, not just the balance','A stock balance says how much should be available; a movement history helps explain why. Receiving, putaway, transfers, retrieval and adjustments should follow defined rules. Record the item, quantity, source, destination and responsible operation as appropriate so discrepancies can be investigated.'),
('Match the physical workflow','Software steps should reflect how warehouse staff receive, locate and move goods. Barcode integration is useful when identifiers and scanning practices are consistent. Before implementation, check the location model, label conventions and common exceptions with the people doing the work.'),
('Use cycle counts to investigate differences','Cycle counting helps compare recorded inventory with physical stock in smaller, manageable groups. Define how discrepancies are reviewed and who can approve an adjustment. A count is most useful when the team can trace the transactions that explain a difference and improve the underlying process.'),
('Coordinate connected systems','ERP and transport integrations need clear ownership of records and events. Decide when a movement is confirmed, how failures are retried and how repeated messages are recognized. Inventory accuracy depends on disciplined workflows and reliable integration behavior; software alone does not guarantee a particular accuracy rate.')])
post('before-building-a-saas-product','Before you build a SaaS product, answer these questions','SaaS Development','Clarify your first customer, core workflow, tenant model and support needs before starting SaaS development.',[
('Who needs the first version?','Describe a specific customer and the problem they experience today. Understand what they currently use, how often the problem occurs and who decides to adopt a new tool. A focused customer profile makes feature decisions easier and keeps the first release connected to a real workflow.'),
('What is the smallest complete workflow?','A minimum release should still let a customer finish something useful. Define the beginning, the steps and the outcome of that workflow. Remove features that are interesting but not necessary to complete it. Establish acceptance criteria in language both the business and developer can assess.'),
('How will organizations and access work?','Decide whether customers are individuals or organizations, how people are invited and which roles they need. Clarify what happens when a user belongs to more than one organization. Tenant boundaries, onboarding and billing are connected product decisions that are expensive to leave ambiguous.'),
('Who will operate and support it?','Plan the responsibilities for deployment, backups, customer support and changes after launch. Determine what staff need to investigate an issue without unnecessary access to customer data. A SaaS product is an ongoing service, so the first-release plan should include its operation as well as its development.')])

